diagnosis_text:
I tested end-to-end and the inter-rack fabric is operating normally. From pc_0_0 to pc_0_1 and back, reachability is 0% loss with ~1.1–1.3 ms RTT, so host-to-host traffic is currently flowing. On the “between the racks” devices, the leafs have the correct routes: leaf_router_0_0 learns 10.0.1.0/24 via BGP on eth0 toward the spines, and leaf_router_0_1 learns 10.0.0.0/24 likewise. Both spines show established BGP adjacencies to the super-spine and both leaves, with prefixes received and sent, and all spine-facing interfaces are up. This confirms the L3 underlay and control plane are converged.

Mechanism: If there had been a spine failure, the leaves would have lost a BGP path and you would see packet loss or longer convergence; instead, multiple BGP neighbors are up and data plane pings succeed, so timeouts cannot be reproduced.

Localization: No faults found on spine_router_0_0, spine_router_0_1, or the attached leaves; all relevant interfaces (eth0/eth1/eth2) are UP and routing is present.

Root cause: Not reproducible—no failed spine or routing outage is present at the time of investigation.

Corrective fix: No changes required. Please retry your jobs. If the issue recurs, capture approximate timestamps; we will review FRR neighbor logs and interface counters for transient flaps and can add monitoring to alert on spine or BGP session drops. === END ===